WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.980
Defensive API design enforces boundaries on both input and output.

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:09.800
Input validation protects the application from malformed or inappropriate requests.

3
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:16.620
Output shaping protects callers and users from receiving data they do not need, should not see, or might misuse.

4
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.560
Both directions matter.

5
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.480
Do not return more data than the caller needs.

6
00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:30.220
It is common for internal objects to contain fields that are useful for storage, auditing, operations, or later workflow steps.

7
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.640
Those fields should not automatically become API response fields.

8
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.500
Defensive serialization selects the response shape deliberately.

9
00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:42.220
Authorization should be checked close to the protected action.

10
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:53.440
An earlier layer may authenticate a user, load a session, or apply a route-level guard, but the code that performs the sensitive operation should still be clear about what permission is required.

11
00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:58.100
Relying only on distant assumptions makes later refactors risky.

12
00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.500
Internal modules also need clear contracts.

13
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.000
Internal does not mean risk-free.

14
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:16.500
A background worker, service object, repository, or utility function can still receive stale data, a partial object, or a value that was valid in one context but not another.

15
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:22.440
Defensive internal contracts describe what the module accepts and what it guarantees.

16
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:25.120
Data flow should be easy to trace.

17
00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:32.740
Engineers should be able to answer where a value came from, whether it was validated, who is allowed to use it, and where it is allowed to go.

18
00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:42.440
This is especially important for identifiers, permissions, account boundaries, personal data, and values that influence security-sensitive decisions.

19
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.840
Defensive data flow reduces accidental exposure and makes code review more effective.

20
00:01:47.940 --> 00:02:00.420
When validation, authorization, transformation, and serialization are visible in the design, reviewers can reason about the system without guessing which earlier layer might have handled a critical step.
